@PACK Survival Guide
@ENTRY rule3|The Rule of Threes
You can survive roughly 3 minutes without air, 3 hours without shelter in harsh weather, 3 days without water, and 3 weeks without food.

This sets your priorities. Most people worry about food first, but exposure and dehydration kill far sooner. Handle air, then shelter, then water, then food, in that order.

Panic wastes the resource you have least of: time and clear thinking. Stop, breathe, and plan.
@END
@ENTRY staysafe|STOP: Your First Move
S - Stop. Sit down and do not make things worse by rushing.
T - Think. What do you have, what do you need, what are the dangers?
O - Observe. Weather, terrain, daylight left, your own condition.
P - Plan. Decide on shelter, water, and signaling before you act.

The biggest survival tool is the mind. Most rescues happen within 72 hours, so your job is to stay alive and findable, not to hike out blindly.
@END
@ENTRY shelter|Emergency Shelter
Shelter protects you from wind, rain, cold, and sun, which drain body heat fast. Build before dark and before you are exhausted.

A lean-to against a ridgepole, covered with branches and debris, sheds rain and blocks wind. Pile a thick bed of dry leaves underneath; the ground steals more heat than the air.

In cold, smaller is warmer: build just big enough for your body. Insulate below you first.
@END
@ENTRY water|Finding and Treating Water
Look for moving water, rain catchment, or morning dew wiped with cloth. Avoid stagnant pools. Follow valleys downhill; water collects low.

Treat everything you cannot trust. Boiling for one minute kills pathogens. If you cannot boil, a cloth filter removes debris but not germs; chemical tablets or a proper filter are safer.

Never ration water to the point of dehydration. Drink when thirsty; a clear head matters more than a full bottle.
@END
@ENTRY fire|Fire Without Matches
Friction methods (bow drill) work but are hard and need dry wood. A ferro rod thrown in your kit is far more reliable; scrape it into a tinder nest of fine dry material.

A magnifying lens, eyeglasses, or even a clear water-filled bag can focus sunlight onto tinder on a bright day.

Protect your fire from wind and rain, and gather three times the wood you think you need before dark.
@END
@ENTRY signaling|Signaling for Rescue
Three of anything means distress: three whistle blasts, three fires in a triangle, three flashes. Sound and light travel farther than your voice.

A signal mirror flash can be seen for miles; aim it toward aircraft and search lines. Bright clothing, ground symbols stamped in snow or laid in rocks, and smoke by day all help searchers find you.

Stay near your last known location if possible. Moving makes you harder to find.
@END
@ENTRY food|Finding Food
Food is the lowest priority in a short emergency, but energy helps morale. Do not eat anything you cannot positively identify; many plants and mushrooms are deadly.

Insects, when cooked, are widely edible and abundant. Avoid brightly colored or hairy ones. Fishing and simple snares can work if you will be out for days.

When in doubt, go without. Starvation takes weeks; a wrong berry can take hours.
@END
@ENTRY cold|Cold Weather Survival
Hypothermia begins with shivering and clumsy hands, then confusion. It can happen above freezing in wet wind. Stay dry; wet clothing loses most of its insulation.

Layer clothing and remove layers before you sweat. Cover your head and neck. Keep moving gently to generate heat, but do not exhaust yourself.

Build insulated shelter off the ground and share body heat if you are with others.
@END
@ENTRY heat|Hot Weather Survival
Heat exhaustion brings heavy sweating, weakness, and nausea; heat stroke stops sweating and brings hot dry skin and confusion, a true emergency. Cool the person immediately.

Travel at dawn and dusk, rest in shade at midday. Cover skin to slow water loss rather than stripping down. Conserve sweat, not water; drink what you have.

Dig a few inches down for cooler soil and shade your shelter from direct sun.
@END
@ENTRY firstaid|Wilderness First Aid
Far from help, infection and immobility are the real dangers, not the injury itself. Control bleeding with firm direct pressure, then clean every wound with treated water and cover it; a small cut that festers days from a road becomes a true emergency.

Splint suspected breaks and sprains before moving; a padded, rigid splint tied above and below the injury turns a crippling injury into a manageable one. For the full step-by-step drills on bleeding, CPR, shock, and breaks, the First Aid guide in GuideBooks is your reference.

The wilderness twist is that you may be both patient and rescuer, and help may be hours or days out. Prevent problems: watch footing, stay hydrated, keep wounds clean, and never push an injured person faster than they can safely go.
@END
@ENTRY animals|Animals and Hazards
Most wild animals want nothing to do with you. Make noise as you travel so you don't surprise anything, store food away from where you sleep (hung or in a hard container), and keep a clean camp; a messy camp draws rodents, bears, and trouble.

If you meet a large animal, do not run, running triggers a chase. Back away slowly, make yourself look big, and speak in a low firm voice. Give any animal a clear escape route; a cornered animal is the dangerous one. Learn the specific advice for your region's wildlife before you go.

Small hazards hurt more people than big ones: know the local venomous snakes, ticks that carry disease, and plants like poison ivy ("leaves of three, let it be"). Check for ticks daily, watch where you put hands and feet, and treat every scrape before it becomes an infection.
@END
@ENTRY navigation|Finding Your Way Out
If you must travel, pick a clear handrail to follow: a river downstream, a ridge line, or a road. Mark your path so searchers can track you and you do not circle.

The sun rises east, sets west. A stick's shadow moves; the tip travels roughly west to east. At night, the North Star sits above the horizon due north in the northern hemisphere.

But remember: in most cases, staying put with shelter and signals gets you rescued faster than wandering.
@END
